[tool_use] Edit {"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/home/aaron/dev/projects/aaron/pointfight-reactor/artifacts/decision-memo.md","old_string":"### Acceptance criteria\n- Phone on a coffee table at rest emits **zero** commit events for 60\n  seconds.\n- Phone strapped to wrist or held: a sharp forward-snap motion emits\n  exactly one commit event within 50ms of the impulse peak.\n- Calibration screen captures 5 samples and persists the threshold;\n  re-running calibration overwrites the prior value.\n- Android: works over HTTP without prompts (Chrome doesn't gate\n  `devicemotion`).\n- iOS: with mkcert HTTPS, \"Tap to enable motion\" grants permission\n  and commits flow.\n- Documented in `docs/SETUP_IOS.md`; iOS acceptance is doc-driven, not\n  CI-driven.","new_string":"### Acceptance criteria\n(See §B3 above for the falsifiable rewrite. Restated here for the builder.)\n\n- 60s at rest on a flat surface → **0** commit events.\n- 5 deliberate forward-snap impulses → exactly **5** commit events\n  (verified from the laptop's debug surface inherited from 2b.1).\n- Double-snap within the 300ms debounce window → exactly **1** commit.\n- Calibration: 5 sample swings → threshold persists in storage;\n  re-running calibration overwrites the prior value; threshold\n  survives a page reload.\n- Android Chrome over plain HTTP: works without prompts (Chrome does\n  not gate `devicemotion`).\n- iOS Safari: `docs/SETUP_IOS.md` (new, authored here) documents the\n  mkcert path. iOS acceptance is doc-driven, not CI-gated (per §B4)."}
